WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:05.060
As we start to better understand the coronavirus,
health experts are worried it could rival

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:08.280
the most devastating health
crises in recent decades.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:13.270
Patients with coronavirus may have mild or
no symptoms and still be spreading it.

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:16.320
They have no fevers, no cough.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.440
You could be unknowingly, unwittingly,
unintentionally spreading this.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:26.760
To add to this it’s believed the coronavirus
is more contagious than seasonal flu.

00:00:26.769 --> 00:00:31.779
Any method of disrupting the cycle of infection
is worthy of our attention.

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:36.610
Scientists in London who’ve had success
in detecting malaria have an unlikely method

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.800
they think could be used in preventing the
spread of COVID-19.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:46.520
When you have an infection your body odor,
the smells coming from your body, change.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.500
Which is detectable by mosquitoes.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.840
So mosquitoes find you more attractive when
you have a malaria infection.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.700
We wondered whether dogs could do the same.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.280
The amazing thing about dogs is they've got
an incredible sense of smell,

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.840
they've got a very sensitive nose.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.140
But they are also able to learn
and they can learn smells.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:11.280
So by combining the two we were able to train dogs

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.240
to be able to detect the smell of malaria

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.420
very effectively above the World Health Organization

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.180
standards for malaria diagnostic.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:29.020
So there is a very, very good chance that
COVID-19 also has a distinctive odor.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:33.880
And if it does, then I am really
confident that the dogs

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:38.640
would be able to learn that
smell and detect it.

00:01:38.649 --> 00:01:42.290
And how do you think it could revolutionize
our response to COVID-19,

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:44.260
having that kind of test available?

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:49.340
One of the main benefits of it would be that
we can rapidly find

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.880
people who have an infection
but don't have any symptoms.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.180
So normally we screen people
for malaria when they present

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:01.440
with the symptoms of malaria
and find out if they have it.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.040
What dogs would be able to do
with odors is find people

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.180
who are showing
no symptoms.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:11.400
So those are the people who will
carry on a cycle of infection.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.660
This is in it’s very early stages, do you
have a time frame?

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.340
So we're hoping that
within two to three months

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:21.260
we would have dogs
actually deployed and being

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.860
out there working to detect.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:26.180
It's going to be really important that we
can monitor people

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.880
coming in and out of the country,
of any country really,

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:34.420
that is potentially infected.
So a screening process to allow

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:37.260
us to do that I think would be very useful,

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.680
This is really a form of screening
a large number of people.

